  "levels": {
    "1": {
      "title": "A new shield for Linux",
      "text": "OpenAI and Cisco made a new tool. It is called Shirudo. It is free for all to use. AI agents write small shields for Linux computers. A shield blocks an attack before the real fix arrives. The computer does not need to stop. In a test, a shield stopped an attack on a service. The team wants AI to fight AI."
    },
    "2": {
      "title": "OpenAI and Cisco launch a tool to shield Linux",
      "text": "OpenAI and Cisco launched Shirudo, a free tool for Linux. The name means \"shield\" in Japanese. AI agents use it to write small shields that block attacks while a computer keeps working.\n\nA fix for a serious problem can take days, weeks or even months to reach most systems. A shield helps during that time. Before it starts to block, the team can watch how it affects the service. A person must approve it, and the shield is removed when the real fix arrives.\n\nIn a test, a shield written by Codex stopped an attack on a running service without a restart. The companies say they want to fight AI attacks with AI."
    },
    "3": {
      "title": "OpenAI and Cisco unveil an AI tool to block exploits before patching",
      "text": "OpenAI and Cisco have launched Shirudo, an open-source Linux runtime that lets AI agents protect systems without any downtime. The name means \"shield\" in Japanese, and the companies describe the project as a first step toward self-defending infrastructure.\n\nThe tool uses eBPF, a technology that runs small, sandboxed programs inside the Linux kernel without changing the system or restarting it. According to Cisco, agents read open-source code and vendor disclosures, trace each flaw to the affected systems, and write a small, targeted shield. The shield only starts blocking attacks when the evidence shows it is safe, and it is retired once the real patch arrives. Operators must approve every shield, and each one is cryptographically signed.\n\nIn a controlled test, a shield generated by Codex stopped an exploit attempt against a running Elasticsearch service without a restart. Cisco's Thomas Graf and Liz Rice argued that this speed is needed because AI models are getting better at finding and chaining flaws, while fixes can still take days or months to reach most systems."
    }
  },
